p('An 88-vertex totient realization of the four-stage contracted nanostar','Title2')
p('DRAFT RESEARCH MANUSCRIPT | 4 November 2026','Small2')
p('Prepared with AI assistance (OpenAI Codex). No human authorship or institutional affiliation is asserted. This is a draft for review; no publication, priority claim, or campaign acceptance is asserted.','Small2')
p('<b>Abstract.</b> We give an explicit positive-integer labeling of the four-stage nanostar tree as an iterated Euler-totient graph. Its orbit graph has 88 vertices and centre 1024. This refutes the final nonexistence conjecture of Ghanbari and Alikhani at stage 4, under the contracted-hexagon interpretation of the nanostar recursion. That interpretation is supported by the published figure and the cited Deutsch-Klavzar construction; it is not an explicit contraction definition in the source. A complete arithmetic table, independent finite verification, and a byte-identical previously checked Lean packet accompany this draft. The contribution is a modest exact counterexample found by routine inverse-totient backtracking.')
head('1. Definitions and the source conjecture')
p('For a positive integer n let phi(n) be Euler\'s totient, with phi(1) = 1. Define phi^0(n) = n and phi^(j+1)(n) = phi(phi^j(n)). For a set A of positive integers put A_phi = {phi^j(a) : a in A, j &gt;= 0}. In particular, iteration zero is included, and 1 belongs to every nonempty orbit set. Let G_phi(A) have vertex set A_phi and unordered edges {n, phi(n)} with n in A_phi and n != phi(n). We omit the loop at 1 and forget direction, in accordance with the source\'s simple-graph convention and explicit undirected remark [1, p. 265].')
p('A graph H is a G_phi-graph when it equals G_phi(A) after labeling its vertices, equivalently when it is isomorphic to G_phi(A). This explains the source\'s notation H = G_phi(A). Although the source allows arbitrary sets A, a finite realization has A finite because A is contained in A_phi. Thus finite positive witnesses suffice here.')
p('The published final conjecture [1, p. 271], quoted verbatim with mathematical notation rendered as G_phi, is: <i>“There is no nanostar molecule which has grown at least four stages, as a G_phi-graph.”</i> The preprint uses the title <i>A graph related to Euler phi function</i>; the published title inserts “the.” The MathDB record 349177 paraphrases “dendrimer nanostar”; the exact frozen raw line and its hash are in the dossier.')
p('Immediately before the conjecture, Theorem 11 states: <i>“The graph D_2 is a G_phi-graph.”</i> Its proof gives A = {3, 256, 376, 384, 564}. We reproduce and check this control, rather than infer impossibility at stage 3 from the conjecture\'s stage threshold. The preprint numbers the corresponding theorem and conjecture 3.4 and 3.5.')
page()
head('2. Which nanostar tree is meant?')
p('Deutsch and Klavzar [2, Section 4.3] define a rooted graph F using two hexagons and endpoint edges, G_1 using a hexagon and a pendant root edge, G_k as a bouquet of G_(k-1), G_(k-1), and F at their roots, and D_k as a bouquet of three G_k. Here bouquet means identification of the specified roots; G_k is rooted at its remaining unique leaf. We interpret the tree structure by contracting each hexagon to one vertex, retaining junction vertices and intervening edges. Published Figure 10 [1, p. 271] and Figure 12 [2, p. 639] have the same contracted skeleton. In the latter the circles represent hexagons. Frozen page images permit direct comparison.')
p('This contraction is our stated interpretation, not a sentence attributed to [1]. A cyclic molecular reading cannot give the intended G_phi-graph: nonempty positive-integer orbit graphs are trees. Indeed phi(n) &lt; n for n &gt; 1, and every vertex reaches 1. In a purported cycle its largest label would have two smaller neighbors, but only its single totient parent can be smaller. In particular, keeping molecular hexagons would already contradict Theorem 11. The distinct D_3[n] family in [3] is not silently substituted for the D_k recursion.')
p('After contraction, F is a rooted path of length 3 and G_1 is a rooted edge. Removing the common central root gives the following recursive arms: arm(0) is one vertex; arm(j+1) starts with two unary path vertices, followed by a branching vertex carrying two disjoint copies of arm(j). For k &gt;= 1, D_k consists of a new centre joined to the roots of three copies of arm(k-1). This is precisely the family used below and in the formal definitions.')
p('If a_j is the order of arm(j), then a_0 = 1 and a_(j+1) = 3 + 2a_j, so a_j = 4*2^j - 3. Hence |D_k| = 1 + 3a_(k-1) = 6*2^k - 8. In D_4 branching occurs at distances 3, 6, and 9 from the centre; leaves occur at distance 10. The distance-layer profile is 1, 3, 3, 3, 6, 6, 6, 12, 12, 12, 24.')
head('3. Explicit refutation and verification')
p('<b>Theorem.</b> Under this contracted-hexagon interpretation, D_4 is a G_phi-graph. Consequently the assertion that no D_k with k &gt;= 4 is realizable is false.')
p('Take the following 23 generators A (all positive):')
p('{'+numbers(r['D4']['generators'])+'}.','Small2')
p('<b>Proof.</b> Tables 1a and 1b give a set V of distinct labels and every totient value, computed by phi(n) = product of p^(e-1)(p-1) over the prime powers p^e in n. Each factorization is prime-certified by trial division. Every printed parent is in V; phi(1) = 1 is the only omitted loop. Following these parents from the listed generators visits exactly V. Thus V = A_phi, not merely a closed superset, and the table determines every edge of G_phi(A).','Small2')
page()
